                'reported on Monday 16 November 2026 that the agency is also tracking two cyclone seeds far from Sumba.</p><h2>Which waters around Sumba BMKG lists</h2><p>The NTT warning names nine '
                'areas in the moderate category (1.25 to 2.5 m): the southern Sape Strait, the western Sumba Strait, the Savu Sea, the waters south of Sumba, the waters north and south of Sabu and '
                'Raijua, the waters north of Kupang and Rote, the waters south of Timor and Rote, and the Komodo National Park waters. Winds across NTT blow from the southeast to the southwest at 6 '
                "to 22 knots, strongest in the Sumba Strait, the Savu Sea and several other channels. BMKG's separate forecast for the western Sumba Strait, valid from 14 to 17 November, flagged "
                'moderate waves of about 1.8 m. The warning page lists no NTT area in the high category (2.5 to 4 m).</p><h2>Two cyclone seeds, far from Sumba</h2><p>A cyclone seed (<i>bibit '
                'siklon</i>) is a low-pressure system that can develop into a tropical cyclone. According to ANTARA, BMKG is watching seed 92B in the Bay of Bengal (1007 hPa, maximum wind 25 knots) '
                "and seed 96S in the Indian Ocean west of Sumatra (1008 hPa). Agie Wandala Putra, BMKG's acting director of maritime meteorology, said they change wind patterns and can raise waves "
                "up to 4 metres between 16 and 19 November 2026. BMKG's NTT text ties local conditions to wind speeds in the channels, not to the seeds. Neither source mentions a cyclone threat for "
                'Sumba.</p><h2>Background: a rainy season that is slow to arrive</h2><p>The island is still waiting for steady rain. As we reported, BMKG expected El Niño to delay the rainy season '
                'in most of NTT until December; see <a href="/en/living/el-nino-late-rainy-season-ntt-sumba-2026/">our report on the delayed rains</a>.</p><h2>What it means for visitors, residents '
                'and investors</h2><p>Visitors: moderate waves are not a ban on sailing, but small boats feel them first. Before a ferry, boat trip, dive or south-coast surf session between 17 and '
